      "narration": [
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-01",
          "text": "A message looks like one piece, but the internet can carry it as several smaller packets."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-02",
          "text": "Here, the sender divides its data into three numbered pieces, each with the same destination."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-03",
          "text": "Routers forward those packets, and they can take different paths through the network."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-04",
          "text": "One packet takes a shorter route, while another spends longer waiting along the way."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-05",
          "text": "That means the pieces may arrive in a different order from the order they were sent."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-06",
          "text": "With TCP, the receiver uses sequence numbers to put the data back into the right order."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-07",
          "text": "Only after the pieces are assembled can the application use the complete message."
        },
        {
          "id": "scene-01-line-08",
          "text": "The useful idea is simple: split, transport, then reassemble."
        }
      ]
    }
